5ba813d69fbde0cc453463b14d4b3","kind":"ARTIFACT","path":"/Users/ev/Projects/mihver/.project/run-bundles/authorization-ledger-v1c-r3-architecture-v4/evidence/shadow-vote-assessment-seat-openai-dae07f340ec6346cfaa517465cad1194f1084dc5f5090d5a550b309131858788.json","producedBy":{"role":"REVIEWER","threadId":null,"tool":"shadow-council-harness"},"sourcePath":"/Users/ev/Projects/mihver/.project/run-bundles/authorization-ledger-v1c-r3-architecture-v4/evidence/shadow-vote-assessment-seat-openai-dae07f340ec6346cfaa517465cad1194f1084dc5f5090d5a550b309131858788.json","summary":"The architecture is close, but two implementation-critical ambiguities prevent approval. First, stopEpoch is specified as an unsigned 64-bit SQLite value with SQL old+1 arithmetic, yet SQLite INTEGER is signed 64-bit; the schema, encoding, comparison, guarded increment, and actual maximum are therefore not implementable exactly as written. Second, SO_PEERCRED supplies the peer’s effective UID/GID, not a complete supplementary-group authorization set; merely naming privileged Unix groups does not define a mechanically reliable per-RPC membership check for typical human accounts. Specify a signed nonnegative range or fixed-width encoding with exact increment semantics, and define authorization through dedicated effective-GID processes, UID allowlists, or verified /proc peer groups. The zero-effect-consumer and fail-closed persistence boundaries otherwise satisfy the decisive V1C requirements."}],"evidenceManifestHash":"sha256:532d8f8bc958e959af0771618c32ea7f132b275b44aada51bb27d6d75f8659d4"}
